1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:10,000
Section 3 You will hear two students called Trudy and Stuart, who are both studying to be librarians,

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
discussing a paper that they are going to write together.

3
00:00:15,632 --> 00:00:51,000
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 23.

4
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:58,000
Okay Stuart, we need to start planning our paper on public libraries.

5
00:00:58,059 --> 00:01:00,000
Have you thought of an angle yet?

6
00:01:00,056 --> 00:01:05,680
Well, there's so much we could look into how libraries have changed over the centuries, for instance,

7
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:08,348
or how different countries organise them.

8
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:10,640
What do you think, Trudy?

9
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:16,384
Maybe we should concentrate on this country and try and relate the changes in libraries to external developments,

10
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:20,400
like the fact that far more people can read than a century ago,

11
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,720
and that the local population may speak lots of different languages.

12
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:28,000
We could include something about changes in the source of funding too.

13
00:01:28,055 --> 00:01:35,000
Yes, but remember we're only supposed to write a short paper, so it's probably best if we don't go into funding in any detail.

14
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:35,627
Right.

15
00:01:35,716 --> 00:01:41,000
Well, should we just brainstorm a few ideas to get started?

16
00:01:41,061 --> 00:01:41,363
Okay.

17
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:46,426
We obviously need to look at the impact of new technology, particularly the internet.

18
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:52,037
Now that lots of books have been digitalised, people can access them from their own computers at home.

19
00:01:52,102 --> 00:01:55,596
And if everyone did that, libraries would be obsolete.

20
00:01:55,661 --> 00:01:55,920
Yes.

21
00:01:55,981 --> 00:02:02,396
But the digitalised books that are available online for free are mostly out of copyright, aren't they?

22
00:02:02,470 --> 00:02:07,436
And copyright in this country lasts for 70 years after the author dies,

23
00:02:07,436 --> 00:02:11,680
so you won't find the latest best seller or up-to-date information.

24
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:13,151
That's an important point.

25
00:02:13,205 --> 00:02:17,648
Anyway, I find it hard to concentrate when I'm reading a long text on a screen.

26
00:02:17,713 --> 00:02:22,800
I'd much rather read a physical book, and it takes longer to read on a screen.

27
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:24,092
Oh, I prefer it.

28
00:02:24,168 --> 00:02:27,360
I suppose it's just a personal preference.

29
00:02:27,422 --> 00:02:31,440
I expect the libraries will go on evolving in the next few years.

30
00:02:31,492 --> 00:02:35,160
Some have already become centres where community activities take place.

31
00:02:35,225 --> 00:02:37,240
Like local clubs meeting there.

32
00:02:37,289 --> 00:02:39,360
I think that will become even more common.

33
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:40,960
I'd like to think so.

34
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:43,840
And that they'll still be serving their traditional function.

35
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:45,000
But I'm not so sure.

36
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:47,635
There are financial implications after all.

37
00:02:47,695 --> 00:02:55,000
What I'm afraid will happen is that books and magazines will all disappear and they'll just be rows and rows of computers.

38
00:02:55,051 --> 00:02:58,000
They won't look anything like the libraries we're used to.

39
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:49,000
Wow, we'll see.

40
00:03:49,043 --> 00:03:53,017
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 24 to 30.

41
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,000
I've just had an idea.

42
00:03:54,043 --> 00:03:58,000
Why don't we make an in-depth study of our local public library as background to our paper?

43
00:03:58,083 --> 00:04:00,417
Yes, that'll be interesting.

44
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,000
And raise all sorts of issues.

45
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:08,000
Let's make a list of possible things we could ask about, then work out some sort of structure.

46
00:04:08,059 --> 00:04:08,824
For instance,

47
00:04:08,824 --> 00:04:16,000
we could interview some of the staff and find out whether the library has its own budget or if that's controlled by the local council.

48
00:04:16,069 --> 00:04:18,000
And what their policies are?

49
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:23,000
I know they don't allow food, but I'd love to find out what types of noise they ban.

50
00:04:23,051 --> 00:04:26,080
There always seems to be a lot of talking, but never music.

51
00:04:26,135 --> 00:04:28,920
I don't know if that's a policy or it just happens.

52
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:30,400
I've often wondered.

53
00:04:30,481 --> 00:04:36,440
Then there are things like how the library is affected by employment laws.

54
00:04:36,497 --> 00:04:40,960
I suppose there are rules about working hours, facilities for staff and so on.

55
00:04:41,074 --> 00:04:41,760
Right.

56
00:04:41,815 --> 00:04:46,846
Then there are other issues relating to the design of the building and how customers use it,

57
00:04:46,846 --> 00:04:50,400
like what measures does the library take to ensure their safety?

58
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:55,600
They'd need floor coverings that aren't slippery and emergency exits, for instance.

59
00:04:55,649 --> 00:05:01,741
Oh, and another thing, there's the question of the kind of insurance the library needs to have in case anyone gets injured.

60
00:05:01,792 --> 00:05:03,810
Yes, that's something else to find out.

61
00:05:03,862 --> 00:05:05,880
You know something I've often wondered?

62
00:05:05,957 --> 00:05:06,880
What's that?

63
00:05:06,933 --> 00:05:10,880
Well, you know they've got an archive of local newspapers going back years.

64
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:16,680
Well, next to it, they've got the diary of a well-known politician from the late 19th century.

65
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:18,680
I wonder why it's there.

66
00:05:18,735 --> 00:05:21,520
Do you know what his connection was with this area?

67
00:05:21,684 --> 00:05:23,000
No idea.

68
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:26,160
Let's add it to our list of things to find out.

69
00:05:26,221 --> 00:05:32,465
I've just thought, you know people might ask in the library about local organisations like sports clubs.

70
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:37,280
Well, I wonder if they keep a database or whether they just look online.

71
00:05:37,334 --> 00:05:37,657
Right.

72
00:05:37,711 --> 00:05:45,117
I quite fancy finding out what the differences are between a library that's open to the public and one that's part of a museum,

73
00:05:45,117 --> 00:05:46,000
for example.

74
00:05:46,069 --> 00:05:48,000
They must be very different.

75
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:53,000
Then something else I'd like to know is...

76
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,000
That is the end of section 3.

77
00:05:57,414 --> 00:06:17,690
You now have half a minute to check your answers.

78
00:06:18,103 --> 00:06:33,000
[BLANK_AUDIO] Now turn to section 4.